INT. NIGHT BUS - MOVING - NIGHT

The 47 night bus, half empty, yellow light flickering over torn seats and a floor sticky with old rain. Windows fogged. A drunk TEENAGER asleep against the glass, another one eating chips from a paper bag, a DOG on a leash tucked under a seat beside its sleeping owner.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three in the morning is when the city tells the truth. Everyone left on this bus is running from somethin or towards it.

In the back row sits RUSS DOLAN, 40s, broad, grey stubble, a gym bag between his boots he keeps touching with one foot. Three rows ahead, NINA FALLOWS, 20s, nurses uniform under a parka, scrolls her phone with a tired thumb.

RUSS
(low, leaning forward, tapping the seat)
Hey. Nina. Dont turn around.

NINA
(not turning, a breath)
Russ. Jesus. I told you, not here.

RUSS
(sliding the bag with his foot)
Your brother says you've got something that belongs to me. Thats all. Hand it over, and I'm off at Carver Street. No fuss.

NINA
(typing, steady)
My brother says alot of things when hes scared.

Two rows up, a young man in a courier jacket, DESMOND PIKE, 30s, takes off his headphones. He has been listening for a while.

DESMOND
(to nobody, mild)
Sorry. Is this a private conversation, or can anyone join?

RUSS
(without looking)
Mind your business, pal.

DESMOND
(smiling, pulling out his own phone)
Ill try. Its difficult. This bus is only forty feet long.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Desmond Pike was a patient man. Desmond Pike was also a cop, and he had been following Russ Dolan for nine weeks.

Across the aisle, an OLD WOMAN with a shopping trolley mutters in Polish and crosses herself. The driver, MAUREEN, 50s, glances in the mirror and turns the radio down a notch.

MAUREEN
(over the intercom, flat)
Next stop Carver Street. Mind the doors please.

NINA
(finally turning, quiet)
Russ, theres a pen drive in my left pocket and its staying there. You want it, you can explain to the man in the courier jacket why.

RUSS
(freezing, tight)
What man.

DESMOND
(raising a hand, cheerful)
Hi.

The bus brakes, hissing. Chips-teenager drops the bag.

RUSS
(low, almost kind)
You wired her, did you. Her. A nurse.

DESMOND
(standing, badge out, slow)
Nobodys wired, Russ. Sit down. Everyone else is going to get off at Carver, and then you and me and Nina are going to ride to the end of the line.

RUSS
(reaching into the bag)
No. No, I dont think we are.

The OLD WOMAN screams. The dog barks, the owner jerks awake. Maureen slams the brakes and the whole bus lurches, bodies thrown, the bag skidding across the floor and spilling, not money, but a long black shape wrapped in a towel.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It always ends in the aisle. Every story on a night bus ends in the aisle.

NINA
(shouting, diving low)
Gun! Desmond, gun!

DESMOND
(drawing, steady)
Russ. Put it down. Put it down now.

RUSS
(unwrapping the towel, trembling)
She took everything. Everything. You think I wanted this?

The overhead light dies. The bus is dark except for the streetlamps stuttering past the windows, and in the pulse of them nobody moves, nobody breathes, and the dog will not stop barking.
